The power dissipated in the field winding is simply the electrical power converted to heat in that resistance:

\[
P_{\text{field}} = \frac{V^{2}}{R}
\]

where  

- \(V = 220\ \text{V}\) (applied voltage)  
- \(R = 110\ \Omega\) (field‑winding resistance)

\[
P_{\text{field}} = \frac{(220\ \text{V})^{2}}{110\ \Omega}
               = \frac{48\,400\ \text{V}^{2}}{110\ \Omega}
               = 440\ \text{W}
\]

\[
\boxed{P_{\text{field}} = 440\ \text{watts}}
\]

The power drawn by the shunt‑field winding is simply the electrical loss in that resistance:

\[
P = \frac{V^{2}}{R} = \frac{(220\text{ V})^{2}}{110\;\Omega}
      = \frac{48\,400}{110}
      = 440\ \text{W}
\]

(You could also get the same result by first finding the field current \(I = V/R = 220/110 = 2\text{ A}\) and then \(P = V I = 220 \times 2 = 440\ \text{W}\).)

**Therefore, the field excitation consumes about 440 W of power.**

