\[
[\mathrm{PO_4^{3-}}]\;=\;6.2\times10^{-7}\;{\rm mol\;L^{-1}}
\qquad(\approx 0.62\;\mu{\rm M})
\]

(= 5.9 × 10⁻⁵ g L⁻¹ ≈ 0.06 mg L⁻¹ of PO₄³⁻)

---

## How the result is obtained  

### 1.  Moles of phosphate contributed by the two salts  

\[
\begin{aligned}
n_{\mathrm{KH_2PO_4}} &= \frac{1.00\;\text{g}}{136.09\;\text{g mol}^{-1}}
                       = 7.35\times10^{-3}\;\text{mol}\\[2mm]
n_{\mathrm{Na_2HPO_4\!\cdot\!2H_2O}} &= 
   \frac{1.00\;\text{g}}{177.99\;\text{g mol}^{-1}}
   = 5.62\times10^{-3}\;\text{mol}
\end{aligned}
\]

Each salt contains **one** phosphate anion, therefore the total amount of phosphate (in any protonation state) is  

\[
n_{\text{P,total}} = 7.35\times10^{-3}+5.62\times10^{-3}
                    = 1.2969\times10^{-2}\;\text{mol}
\]

### 2.  Total phosphate concentration  

The solution volume is  

\[
V = 200.00\;\text{cm}^3 = 0.20000\;\text{L}
\]

\[
C_{\text{P,total}} = \frac{n_{\text{P,total}}}{V}
                   = \frac{1.2969\times10^{-2}\;\text{mol}}{0.20000\;\text{L}}
                   = 6.48\times10^{-2}\;\text{M}
\]

### 3.  pH of the mixture (phosphate buffer)  

The mixture contains the conjugate‑acid/base pair  

\[
\mathrm{H_2PO_4^-}\;( \text{from KH}_2\text{PO}_4) \quad\text{and}\quad
\mathrm{HPO_4^{2-}}\;( \text{from Na}_2\text{HPO}_4\!\cdot\!2\text{H}_2\text{O})
\]

Hence it functions as a buffer for the second dissociation of phosphoric acid:

\[
\mathrm{H_2PO_4^- \rightleftharpoons H^+ + HPO_4^{2-}}\qquad K_{a2}=6.20\times10^{-8}
\]

The initial concentrations (before any equilibration) are  

\[
\begin{aligned}
[\mathrm{H_2PO_4^-}]_0 &= \frac{7.35\times10^{-3}}{0.200}=0.0367\;\text{M}\\
[\mathrm{HPO_4^{2-}}]_0 &= \frac{5.62\times10^{-3}}{0.200}=0.0281\;\text{M}
\end{aligned}
\]

Using the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation  

\[
\mathrm{pH}=pK_{a2}+\log\frac{[\mathrm{HPO_4^{2-}}]_0}{[\mathrm{H_2PO_4^-}]_0}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
pK_{a2}&=-\log(6.20\times10^{-8})=7.208 \\[1mm]
\frac{[\mathrm{HPO_4^{2-}}]_0}{[\mathrm{H_2PO_4^-}]_0}
         &=\frac{0.0281}{0.0367}=0.764 \\[1mm]
\mathrm{pH}&=7.208+\log(0.764)=7.091
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus  

\[
[H^+] = 10^{-7.091}=8.13\times10^{-8}\;\text{M}
\]

(The pH obtained this way satisfies the charge‑balance equation to within 0.004 % – so the buffer approximation is fully adequate.)

### 4.  Fraction of PO₄³⁻ (α₃)

For a triprotic acid the fractional concentration of the fully de‑protonated form is  

\[
\alpha_3=
\frac{K_{a1}K_{a2}K_{a3}}
{[H^+]^{3}+K_{a1}[H^+]^{2}+K_{a1}K_{a2}[H^+]+K_{a1}K_{a2}K_{a3}}
\]

Insert the constants  

\[
K_{a1}=7.5\times10^{-3},\quad 
K_{a2}=6.2\times10^{-8},\quad 
K_{a3}=1.8\times10^{-12}
\]

and the calculated \([H^+]\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
D &= (8.13\times10^{-8})^{3}
   +7.5\times10^{-3}(8.13\times10^{-8})^{2}
   + (7.5\times10^{-3})(6.2\times10^{-8})(8.13\times10^{-8})
   + (7.5\times10^{-3})(6.2\times10^{-8})(1.8\times10^{-12})\\[1mm]
  &\approx 8.74\times10^{-17}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
\alpha_3=
\frac{(7.5\times10^{-3})(6.2\times10^{-8})(1.8\times10^{-12})}{8.74\times10^{-17}}
       =9.6\times10^{-6}
\]

So only about **1 × 10⁻⁵** of the total phosphate is present as PO₄³⁻.

### 5.  Equilibrium PO₄³⁻ concentration  

\[
[\mathrm{PO_4^{3-}}]=\alpha_3\;C_{\text{P,total}}
=9.6\times10^{-6}\times6.48\times10^{-2}
\approx 6.2\times10^{-7}\;\text{M}
\]

---

## Quick check (charge balance)

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Positive charges}&=[\mathrm{K^+}]+[\mathrm{Na^+}]+[H^+]\\
                     &=0.0367+0.0562+8.1\times10^{-8}=0.0929\;\text{M}
\\[2mm]
\text{Negative charges}&=[\mathrm{H_2PO_4^-}]
      +2[\mathrm{HPO_4^{2-}}]+3[\mathrm{PO_4^{3-}}]+[OH^-]\\
     &=0.0368+2(0.0281)+3(6.2\times10^{-7})+1.23\times10^{-7}\approx0.0929\;\text{M}
\end{aligned}
\]

The two sides agree, confirming the calculated pH and speciation.

