\[
\boxed{[\mathrm{PO_4^{3-}}]=6.2\times10^{-7}\;\text{mol L}^{-1}
\;(≈0.62\;\mu\text{M})}
\]

**Solution**

The heat loss from the pipe is by steady‑state conduction through two cylindrical layers:

* Stainless‑steel pipe wall (inner radius \(r_1\), outer radius \(r_2\))
* Insulating material (inner radius \(r_2\), outer radius \(r_3\))

The resistance of a cylindrical layer of length \(L\) (here we take \(L=1\;{\rm ft}\)) is  

\[
R=\frac{\ln\!\left(\dfrac{r_{\text{outer}}}{r_{\text{inner}}}\right)}{2\pi kL}
\qquad\left[\frac{\rm hr\;ft\;^{\circ}{\rm F}}{\rm Btu}\right]
\]

---

### 1.   Geometry (feet)

\[
\begin{aligned}
r_1 &= \frac{{\rm ID}}{2}= \frac{6}{2}\;{\rm in}=3\;{\rm in}= \frac{3}{12}=0.250\;{\rm ft}\\[4pt]
r_2 &= \frac{{\rm OD}}{2}= \frac{6.5}{2}\;{\rm in}=3.25\;{\rm in}= \frac{3.25}{12}=0.270833\;{\rm ft}\\[4pt]
\text{Insulation thickness}=3.5\;{\rm in}= \frac{3.5}{12}=0.291667\;{\rm ft}\\[4pt]
r_3 &= r_2+0.291667 =0.270833+0.291667 =0.562500\;{\rm ft}
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 2.   Thermal resistances  

*Pipe steel* (\(k_{st}=26\ {\rm Btu/hr\;ft\;^{\circ}{\rm F}}\))

\[
R_{st}=\frac{\ln\!\left(\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}\right)}{2\pi k_{st}}
      =\frac{\ln\!\bigl(\tfrac{13}{12}\bigr)}{2\pi(26)}
      =\frac{0.080198}{163.363}=4.91\times10^{-4}
      \; \frac{{\rm hr\;ft\;^{\circ}{\rm F}}}{\rm Btu}
\]

*Insulation* (\(k_{ins}=0.04\ {\rm Btu/hr\;ft\;^{\circ}{\rm F}}\))

\[
R_{ins}= \frac{\ln\!\left(\dfrac{r_3}{r_2}\right)}{2\pi k_{ins}}
        =\frac{\ln\!\bigl(\tfrac{27}{13}\bigr)}{2\pi(0.04)}
        =\frac{0.730733}{0.251327}=2.907\;
        \frac{{\rm hr\;ft\;^{\circ}{\rm F}}}{\rm Btu}
\]

*Total resistance per foot*

\[
R_{\text{tot}}=R_{st}+R_{ins}=2.9075\;
\frac{{\rm hr\;ft\;^{\circ}{\rm F}}}{\rm Btu}
\]

---

### 3.   Heat loss per foot  

\[
\Delta T = T_{i}-T_{o}=500^{\circ}{\rm F}-150^{\circ}{\rm F}=350^{\circ}{\rm F}
\]

\[
\boxed{ \; \dot Q \;=\;\frac{\Delta T}{R_{\text{tot}}}
          =\frac{350}{2.9075}=1.20\times10^{2}
          \; \frac{{\rm Btu}}{\rm hr\;ft}\;}
\]

In round numbers  

\[
\dot Q \approx 1.2\times10^{2}\ {\rm Btu/hr\; per\; ft}
\]

---

### 4.   Temperature at the pipe‑insulation interface  

The temperature drop across the steel wall:

\[
\Delta T_{st}= \dot Q\;R_{st}
            = (120.4\ {\rm Btu/hr\;ft})(4.91\times10^{-4})
            \approx 0.059^{\circ}{\rm F}
\]

\[
T_{\text{interface}} = T_{i}-\Delta T_{st}
                     \approx 500.0^{\circ}{\rm F}-0.06^{\circ}{\rm F}
                     \approx 4.99\times10^{2}\ ^{\circ}{\rm F}
\]

\[
\boxed{T_{\text{pipe–insulation interface}}\ \approx\ 5.00\times10^{2}\;^{\circ}{\rm F}}
\]

(The pipe wall resistance is almost negligible compared with the insulation, so the outer surface of the pipe is essentially at the same temperature as the inner surface.)

---

### 5.   Summary  

| Quantity                              | Value |
|--------------------------------------|-------|
| Heat loss per foot of pipe            | **≈ 120 Btu hr⁻¹ ft⁻¹** |
| Outer surface temperature of pipe (pipe‑insulation interface) | **≈ 500 °F** (≈ 0.06 °F lower than the inner surface) |

These results illustrate that for a thin stainless‑steel pipe the thermal resistance of the pipe wall is tiny; virtually all of the temperature drop from 500 °F to 150 °F occurs across the 3.5‑in. thick insulation.
